<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henry II of France</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1519">1519</a>–<a href="/years/1559">1559</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-valois">House of Valois</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henry II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-duke-of-nevers">Francis I</a>, was king of France from 1547 to 1559. As dauphin he was the last separate Duke of Brittany, so his accession merged the duchy's title with the crown. He fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toul">Toul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verdun">Verdun</a> in 1552 and Calais in 1558, and gave the duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valentinois">Valentinois</a> to his mistress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diane-de-poitiers">Diane de Poitiers</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-france.jpg" alt="Henry II of France" width="320" height="438" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Workshop of François Clouet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Henry_II_of_France-Fran%C3%A7ois_Clouet_(altered).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Henry was born on 31 March 1519 at the Château de Saint-Germain-en-Laye, the second son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-duke-of-nevers">Francis I</a> and Claude, Duchess of Brittany. After Francis I was captured at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pavia">Battle of Pavia</a> in 1525, Henry and his elder brother were sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> as hostages and stayed there for over four years. On 28 October 1533 he married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-de-medici">Catherine de' Medici</a>, both being fourteen.</p><p>He formed a close attachment to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diane-de-poitiers">Diane de Poitiers</a>, more than twenty years older than he, whose colours he wore at a tournament in 1531. He became heir in August 1536 when his brother died after falling ill following a game of tennis; an autopsy report gave no sign of poison. Catherine bore their first son in 1544, and Henry's loyalty to Diane caused a breach with his father until 1545.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-duke-of-nevers">Francis I</a> died on 31 March 1547, and Henry was crowned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a> on 25 July 1547. He intensified the repression of Protestants. An edict of 5 April 1547 reaffirmed punishments for blasphemy, and in October a new chamber of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parlement-of-paris">Parlement of Paris</a>, called the chambre ardente, was set up to hear heresy cases. The Edict of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chateaubriant">Châteaubriant</a> followed in 1551.</p><p>In foreign policy he declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-war-of-1551-1559">Italian War of 1551–1559</a>, allied himself with German Protestant princes at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-chambord">Treaty of Chambord</a> in 1552 and continued his father's Franco-Ottoman alliance. He occupied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-bishoprics">Three Bishoprics</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toul">Toul</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verdun">Verdun</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a>, and secured them at Renty in 1554. After Philip II's victory at St Quentin, French forces took Calais.</p><p>Henry raised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-queen-of-scots">Mary, Queen of Scots</a> at his court, and she married the Dauphin Francis on 24 April 1558. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-cateau-cambresis">Peace of Cateau-Cambrésis</a> of April 1559, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> gave up its claims in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a> and Savoy but kept Saluzzo, Calais and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-bishoprics">Three Bishoprics</a>. During his reign Henry also introduced the idea of publishing a description of an invention in exchange for monopoly rights, the beginning of the patent specification.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 30 June 1559, at a tournament held to celebrate the peace, Henry was wounded in the eye by a splinter of the lance of Gabriel Montgomery, captain of his Scottish Guard. Surgeons including Ambroise Paré and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andreas-vesalius">Andreas Vesalius</a> could not save him, and he died ten days later. Modern analysis points to an infection from a retained wooden fragment.</p><p>His death played a part in the decline of jousting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. He left four young sons and a widow, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-de-medici">Catherine de' Medici</a>. Three of the sons became king, but their youth and Catherine's regency led to challenges from powerful nobles and helped to spark the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-wars-of-religion">French Wars of Religion</a>, and in time the Valois dynasty ended.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Henry II of France” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When the <b>Dauphin Henry</b>, duke since his brother’s death in 1536, became Henry II in 1547, the ducal title merged with the crown.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brittany">Duchy of Brittany · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Charles III, raised at the court of <b>Henry II of France</b>, built a more centralised princely state: he extended Nancy with the planned Ville-Neuve from 1588, issued a general codification of Lorraine customary law in 1594 and enlarged the ducal bureaucracy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-lorraine">Duchy of Lorraine · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Louis XII later made it a duchy for Cesare Borgia in 1498, and <b>Henry II</b> gave it to Diane de Poitiers.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-valentinois">County of Valentinois</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1552 <b>Henry II of France</b> occupied Metz under the Treaty of Chambord with German Protestant princes.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-metz">Free Imperial City of Metz</a></li><li><blockquote>After quarrels in the ruling family, King <b>Henry II of France</b> annexed the marquisate in 1548.</blockquote><a href="/state/marquisate-of-saluzzo">Marquisate of Saluzzo</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1552 <b>Henry II of France</b> seized Metz, Toul and Verdun, and the Peace of Westphalia in 1648 confirmed French sovereignty over the Three Bishoprics.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-metz">Prince-Bishopric of Metz</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Château de Saint-Germain-en-Laye, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Hôtel des Tournelles, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.9,2.1&amp;year=1545" aria-label="Château de Saint-Germain-en-Laye on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Henry II of France"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5118 5942h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5118 5942h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.9,2.1&amp;year=1545">What was here in 1545? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
